<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Books and Movies – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Books and Movies</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Favorite Movie Is... Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose short pieces of creative writing. Interactive: Provide feedback on short texts during a peer review session.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose a short, creative piece inspired by a favorite book, using simple details to describe key elements (e.g., "In my version of the story, the animals become friends and start their own adventure"). Can provide feedback on a peer's description of a movie (e.g., "Your story about the movie is interesting"). Can compose a simple review, sharing why a book or movie is enjoyable (e.g., "I read a book about life in the future. It showed the impact of technology in our lives, and robots played an active role").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various movie posters and book covers on the board. Students are asked to quickly jot down (write) two to three words that describe one of the posters/covers that catches their attention, focusing on descriptive adjectives like "entertaining," "engaging," or "emotional." For example, "This movie looks exciting." or "This book seems emotional."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing a simple sentence about a favorite movie or book on the board, demonstrating the use of "like/prefer" and "because." For example, the teacher writes: "I like watching documentaries because they are very engaging and I learn new facts." The teacher explicitly uses vocabulary such as 'movie', 'book', 'entertaining', 'emotional'. The teacher also briefly highlights the pronunciation of the "-s" sound in "watches" and "likes" as appropriate.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I prefer action movies over comedies,' what do I like more?" (Expected answer: Action movies.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When do we use 'because'?" (Expected answer: To give a reason.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides students with a short, simple written text (a descriptive paragraph/mini-review) about a fictional movie or book, specifically crafted to include target grammar (present simple, past simple, "verb + -ing + noun + because," comparing preferences) and vocabulary (movie, book, character, plot, theme, entertaining, engaging, emotional, favorite). The genre is a "short review/descriptive text" with a semi-formal register.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Text:</b> "My favorite movie is 'The Magical Forest'. It's an animated documentary about animals. I like watching it because the plot is very engaging and the main character, a wise owl, is very emotional. I watched it yesterday with my family. It was about how different animals become friends and help each other. I prefer this movie over typical comedies because it's more thrilling and teaches about nature creatively."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand how to compose short pieces of creative writing, specifically simple reviews and descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the provided text individually. Then, they answer simple written comprehension questions:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is the genre of 'The Magical Forest'? (Expected: animated documentary)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What does the author like about the movie? (Expected: plot is engaging, character is emotional)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True or False: The author watched the movie last week. (Expected: False - watched yesterday)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Why does the author prefer this movie over comedies? (Expected: more thrilling, teaches creatively)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using present simple (for preferences and reasons), past simple (for describing what happened), "verb + -ing + noun + because," and comparing preferences. Vocabulary includes 'movie', 'book', 'character', 'plot', 'theme', 'documentary', 'entertaining', 'engaging', 'emotional', 'favorite', 'like', 'prefer', 'watch', 'enjoy'. Sequencers (first, next, after, finally) and adverbs (creatively, emotionally) are encouraged.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Fill-in-the-blanks):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences containing blanks. Students complete the sentences using appropriate target vocabulary and grammar structures.
                        <br><i>Examples:</i>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I _______ (like/prefer) _______ (watch) _______ (action movies) _______ (because) they are very _______ (thrilling).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Yesterday, I _______ (watch) a _______ (comedy). It _______ (be) about a funny _______ (character) and his crazy _______ (plot).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">She _______ (read) a _______ (book) about a sad _______ (theme). It makes her feel _______ (emotional).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Sentence Building):</b> Students are given prompts (e.g., noun + adjective + verb + because) and must write complete sentences about books or movies. They can choose from a bank of target words.
                        <br><i>Examples of prompts:</i>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Favorite book / engaging / plot / relax) → "My favorite book has an engaging plot, so I enjoy reading it to relax."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Watched movie / yesterday / funny / character / laugh) → "I watched a movie yesterday. It had a funny character that made me laugh."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Prefer / poetry / emotional / express) → "I prefer reading poetry because it helps to express emotional feelings."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Check and Correction):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes 3-4 sentences describing a different movie or book they like, using at least one past simple sentence and one "because" clause. Then, they exchange their written pieces. Each student *reads* their partner's sentences and *writes* one constructive piece of feedback or correction (e.g., "Good use of 'because'!" or "Check the past tense of 'watch' here.") on a sticky note or on the paper itself. This integrates providing constructive feedback.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task:</b> Students will individually write a short creative review or descriptive paragraph (5-7 sentences) about their favorite movie or book, focusing on the theme "My Favorite Movie Is... Because...".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual creative writing composition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose one favorite movie or book from the "Books and Movies" scenario.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They compose a paragraph describing their choice, explaining why they like it. They must include:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The title and genre (e.g., comedy, documentary, fiction).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One detail about a character or plot element.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least two reasons why they enjoy it, using "because" and descriptive adjectives (e.g., "entertaining," "engaging," "emotional").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Optionally, they can compare it to another genre or type of media (e.g., "I prefer action movies over comedies because they are more thrilling.").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encourage the use of sequencers (first, next, after, finally) to organize their ideas if describing a sequence of events or reasons.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in written expression, using the target language to compose a coherent and descriptive text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer assessment using a simple checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their written reviews from Stage 4 with a new partner. Each student reads their peer's review and uses a simple checklist to provide constructive written feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: square; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Does the review include the title and genre? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are there at least two reasons for liking the movie/book, using "because"? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are descriptive adjectives (e.g., entertaining, emotional) used? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are sentences generally clear and easy to understand? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Suggest one improvement: ______________________</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One thing I liked about your review: ______________________</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> After receiving peer feedback, students reflect on their own writing. They write responses to the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one strength of your written review?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one piece of feedback from your peer that you will use to improve your writing?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did you find easy about writing your movie/book review today? What was challenging?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How do you think improving your writing skills can help you in other subjects or in real life?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., student engagement with peer feedback, clarity of instructions, effective use of target grammar/vocabulary).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed for future writing lessons on this topic? (e.g., more sentence starters, explicit modeling of sentence combining, more focus on spelling).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did the students integrate the previously learned vocabulary and speaking skills into their writing?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This writing lesson directly prepares students for the Lesson 5, Mediation & Integration. Their ability to compose descriptive paragraphs and provide feedback will be essential for the 21st Century Skills Project, which may involve summarizing information about books/movies or presenting their recommendations in a clear, structured manner.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>